{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791836487418,"sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","part":{"partID":"prt_131c61624001SUAyvhI0X9c7N7","sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","messageID":"msg_131c610d7001iyvfy6a130dlKs","type":"tool","id":"toolu_vrtx_01A4zvsxZVBQcBNVV5pHMwvC","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2/cases/case-002-event-driven/artifacts/result.md","content":"# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** When a customer submits a payment that the payment\nservice judges to be valid, the payment service must return an authorization\nresult to the caller within 2 seconds.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The payment service — specifically its inbound\npayment submission interface (the boundary at which a customer or calling\nsystem delivers a payment request) and its outbound response (the boundary at\nwhich the payment service returns an authorization result).\n\n**Source classification:**\n\n| Source fragment | Kind |\n|---|---|\n| \"When a customer submits a valid payment\" | Trigger / behavioral obligation scope |\n| \"the payment service shall return an authorization result\" | Behavioral obligation |\n| \"within 2 seconds\" | Timing constraint (normative) |\n\nThe source uses `shall`, which is treated as a normative obligation. The\nphrasing maps directly to the Event-driven EARS pattern. Several terms are\nundefined or ambiguous and block full readiness.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 — Definition of \"valid payment\":** What conditions must a payment request\nsatisfy to be considered valid? For example: presence of required fields,\npassing format checks, passing fraud screening, successful authentication, or\nsome combination. This matters because the trigger fires only on a valid\npayment; the behavior for an invalid submission is currently unspecified.\n\n**Q2 — Definition of \"authorization result\":** What is the observable content\nor structure of an authorization result? At minimum: does it include an\nauthorization code, an approval/decline indicator, or both? Is a partial\nresult (e.g., a pending status) considered a valid authorization result for\nthis requirement?\n\n**Q3 — Measurement point for the 2-second deadline:** Is the 2 seconds\nmeasured from the moment the payment service receives the complete payment\nrequest, to the moment the last byte of the authorization result is sent at\nthe service boundary? Or is it measured end-to-end from the customer's\nperspective? The measurement point is required to make the timing contract\nverifiable.\n\n**Q4 — Behavior when the deadline is exceeded:** If the payment service\ncannot return an authorization result within 2 seconds, what must it do?\nOptions include: return a timeout error response, cancel the authorization\nattempt, or continue processing and return a late result. The source is silent\non this.\n\n**Q5 — Behavior on invalid payment submission:** What shall the payment\nservice return (or do) when a customer submits a payment that is not valid?\nThis is a gap in the source — the 2-second obligation applies only to valid\nsubmissions.\n\n**Q6 — Concurrency and workload scope:** Does the 2-second deadline apply at\nall load levels, or only up to a defined concurrent request rate or transaction\nvolume? An unconditional latency requirement may be infeasible under\npathological load; knowing the intended workload scope makes the timing\nconstraint verifiable.\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a customer submits a valid payment, the payment service\n  shall return an authorization result within 2 seconds.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source requests a bounded-latency\n  guarantee on the normal path of a payment authorization. The 2-second limit\n  implies a real-time or near-real-time user experience goal for payment\n  confirmation.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The payment service inbound submission interface and\n    outbound response interface.\n  - Responsible system: The payment service.\n  - Actor: Customer (or a calling system acting on behalf of a customer).\n  - Affected interfaces: Payment submission interface (inbound); authorization\n    result interface (outbound).\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \"Valid payment\" — **unresolved** (see Q1).\n    \"Authorization result\" — **unresolved** (see Q2).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the discrete\n    event of a customer submitting a payment that satisfies the validity\n    criteria. No precondition beyond validity is stated.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal response is an\n    authorization result returned within 2 seconds. Behavior when the deadline\n    is exceeded is **unresolved** (see Q4). Behavior on invalid submission is\n    **unresolved** (see Q5).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 2 seconds.\n    Measurement point is **unresolved** (see Q3). Applicable workload scope is\n    **unresolved** (see Q6).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Valid payment,\"\n    \"authorization result,\" measurement point, timeout behavior, and invalid\n    submission behavior are all unresolved.\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A well-formed, valid payment\n    request is submitted to the payment service at its inbound interface.\n    Definition of \"valid\" must be resolved before the test can be specified.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The payment service emits an authorization\n    result at its outbound response interface. The elapsed time between\n    receiving the request and emitting the result is recorded.\n  - Expected result: An authorization result is returned; the elapsed time does\n    not exceed 2 seconds.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if\n    an authorization result is observable at the service boundary within 2\n    seconds of receiving a valid payment submission. The exact measurement\n    points are **unresolved** (see Q3).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 2 seconds;\n    behavior when the deadline is exceeded; behavior on invalid input — all\n    **unresolved** (see Q4, Q5).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence:\n    Timestamps at the inbound and outbound service boundary; definition of\n    authorization result structure; agreed workload parameters.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes (timing and response content\n    are both observable at the payment service interface, once terms are\n    defined).\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**SR-1 — Failure-path companion (timeout behavior):**\nRelated to Candidate 1 (timeout on valid payment submission).\nMissing behavior: What the payment service shall return if the 2-second\ndeadline is exceeded before an authorization result is available.\nSuggested draft (provisional, not normative until accepted): \"If the payment\nservice cannot return an authorization result within 2 seconds of receiving a\nvalid payment submission, then the payment service shall return a timeout\nerror response to the caller.\"\nReason: Without this, a verifier cannot distinguish a late response from no\nresponse, and an implementer cannot choose a safe default.\n\n**SR-2 — Failure-path companion (invalid payment response):**\nRelated to Candidate 1 (behavior on invalid payment submission).\nMissing behavior: What the payment service shall return when the submitted\npayment is not valid.\nSuggested draft (provisional): \"When a customer submits a payment that does\nnot satisfy the validity criteria, the payment service shall return a rejection\nresponse that identifies the reason for rejection.\"\nReason: The source scopes the 2-second obligation to valid payments only;\nbehavior on invalid submissions is a material gap.\n\n**SR-3 — Boundary companion (concurrent workload scope):**\nRelated to Candidate 1 (workload scope of the timing constraint).\nMissing behavior: The maximum concurrent load or transaction volume under which\nthe 2-second guarantee applies.\nSuggested draft (provisional): \"The payment service shall return an\nauthorization result within 2 seconds for up to [N] concurrent valid payment\nsubmissions.\" ([N] is a placeholder requiring a product or capacity decision.)\nReason: An unconditional latency guarantee without a workload bound is not\nmeaningfully verifiable and may not be implementable.\n\n**SR-4 — Operational companion (auditability):**\nRelated to Candidate 1 (observability of authorization results).\nMissing behavior: Whether authorization results and their associated timing\nmust be recorded for audit or dispute resolution.\nSuggested draft (provisional): \"The payment service shall record the timestamp\nof each received valid payment submission and the timestamp of each returned\nauthorization result.\"\nReason: A 2-second latency requirement implies the ability to measure and\naudit response time in production; without retained timestamps this may not be\noperationally verifiable.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n**Finding 1 — Gap: No failure path for deadline breach**\nCandidate 1 defines the normal-path obligation (return within 2 seconds) but\nis silent on what occurs if the deadline is not met. This leaves both\nimplementers and verifiers with no normative guidance on timeout behavior.\nResolution options: (a) add SR-1 as an accepted requirement; (b) confirm that\nlate results are acceptable and remove the deadline; (c) confirm that the\ncalling system must handle absence of a response within 2 seconds without a\nservice-side obligation.\n\n**Finding 2 — Gap: Invalid payment behavior undefined**\nThe trigger condition (\"valid payment\") implies a complementary case\n(invalid/unrecognized payment), for which no requirement exists. This is a\nbehavioral gap, not a conflict.\nResolution options: (a) accept SR-2; (b) explicitly state that invalid\nsubmissions are out of scope for this requirement set.\n\n**Finding 3 — Potential vacuousness without workload scope**\nAn unconditional 2-second latency requirement may be infeasible or untestable\nwithout a defined workload ceiling, making it potentially vacuous in\nproduction. This is not a conflict among candidates but a concern about the\nsingle candidate's verifiability.\nResolution options: (a) accept SR-3 to bound the workload; (b) confirm the\nrequirement is intended to apply unconditionally and document the implementer's\nobligation to provision accordingly.\n\nNo conflicts, duplicates, or precedence questions exist among the current\ncandidates because there is only one candidate. The above findings concern\ncompleteness and verifiability.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1** — \"Customer\" is interpreted as any principal (human user or calling\nsystem) that submits a payment to the payment service's submission interface.\nIf customers and calling systems have distinct behavioral obligations, this\nassumption must be revisited.\n\n**A2** — \"Submit\" is interpreted as the discrete event of delivering a\ncomplete payment request to the payment service at its inbound interface. If\nsubmission is a multi-step process (e.g., initiate, confirm, finalize), the\ntrigger point must be specified.\n\n**A3** — The 2-second limit is interpreted as a maximum elapsed time, not a\ntarget or a mean. No statistical tolerance (e.g., 99th percentile) is assumed.\nIf the intent is a percentile-based constraint, the requirement must be\nrevised.\n\n**A4** — \"Return\" is interpreted as the payment service emitting the\nauthorization result at its outbound interface to the caller. Network\ntransmission time beyond the service boundary is not included in the 2-second\nobligation unless Q3 resolves otherwise.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe source sentence establishes a real-time latency guarantee for the\nauthorization path of a payment service. The 2-second bound is a common\nindustry target for interactive payment flows, suggesting a user-experience\nrationale (customers should not wait more than 2 seconds for a payment\ndecision). However, the source does not state this rationale explicitly.\n\nThe requirement is structurally well-formed — it names a system, a trigger,\nand a response with a timing constraint — but several terms are undefined and\nfailure paths are absent. These gaps do not change the intent of the source\nrequest; they are information required to make the requirement implementable\nand verifiable.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n- **Candidate 1:** `needs clarification`\n  Blocking items: Q1 (definition of \"valid payment\"), Q2 (definition of\n  \"authorization result\"), Q3 (measurement point for 2-second deadline), Q4\n  (behavior when deadline is exceeded), Q5 (behavior on invalid payment), Q6\n  (workload scope). The verification contract cannot specify setup, oracle, or\n  pass/fail criteria until these are resolved.\n\n- **SR-1 through SR-4:** Suggested supporting requirements, not yet accepted.\n  Status will be assigned after the user confirms scope.\n\nNo candidates are `ready for review`. All candidates require resolution of the\nclarifying questions before the readiness gate can be re-evaluated.\n"},"output":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2/cases/case-002-event-driven/artifacts/result.md\", \"content\": \"# Requirements Elicitation Package\\n\\n## Understanding\\n\\n**Requested outcome:** When a customer submits a payment that the payment\\nservice judges to be valid, the payment service must return an authorization\\nresult to the caller within 2 seconds.\\n\\n**Proposed system boundary:** The payment service — specifically its inbound\\npayment submission interface (the boundary at which a customer or calling\\nsystem delivers a payment request) and its outbound response (the boundary at\\nwhich the payment service returns an authorization result).\\n\\n**Source classification:**\\n\\n| Source fragment | Kind |\\n|---|---|\\n| \\\"When a customer submits a valid payment\\\" | Trigger / behavioral obligation scope |\\n| \\\"the payment service shall return an authorization result\\\" | Behavioral obligation |\\n| \\\"within 2 seconds\\\" | Timing constraint (normative) |\\n\\nThe source uses `shall`, which is treated as a normative obligation. The\\nphrasing maps directly to the Event-driven EARS pattern. Several terms are\\nundefined or ambiguous and block full readiness.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Clarifying Questions\\n\\n**Q1 — Definition of \\\"valid payment\\\":** What conditions must a payment request\\nsatisfy to be considered valid? For example: presence of required fields,\\npassing format checks, passing fraud screening, successful authentication, or\\nsome combination. This matters because the trigger fires only on a valid\\npayment; the behavior for an invalid submission is currently unspecified.\\n\\n**Q2 — Definition of \\\"authorization result\\\":** What is the observable content\\nor structure of an authorization result? At minimum: does it include an\\nauthorization code, an approval/decline indicator, or both? Is a partial\\nresult (e.g., a pending status) considered a valid authorization result for\\nthis requirement?\\n\\n**Q3 — Measurement point for the 2-second deadline:** Is the 2 seconds\\nmeasured from the moment the payment service receives the complete payment\\nrequest, to the moment the last byte of the authorization result is sent at\\nthe service boundary? Or is it measured end-to-end from the customer's\\nperspective? The measurement point is required to make the timing contract\\nverifiable.\\n\\n**Q4 — Behavior when the deadline is exceeded:** If the payment service\\ncannot return an authorization result within 2 seconds, what must it do?\\nOptions include: return a timeout error response, cancel the authorization\\nattempt, or continue processing and return a late result. The source is silent\\non this.\\n\\n**Q5 — Behavior on invalid payment submission:** What shall the payment\\nservice return (or do) when a customer submits a payment that is not valid?\\nThis is a gap in the source — the 2-second obligation applies only to valid\\nsubmissions.\\n\\n**Q6 — Concurrency and workload scope:** Does the 2-second deadline apply at\\nall load levels, or only up to a defined concurrent request rate or transaction\\nvolume? An unconditional latency requirement may be infeasible under\\npathological load; knowing the intended workload scope makes the timing\\nconstraint verifiable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Candidate Requirements\\n\\n### Candidate 1\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** Event-driven\\n- **Requirement:** When a customer submits a valid payment, the payment service\\n  shall return an authorization result within 2 seconds.\\n\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source requests a bounded-latency\\n  guarantee on the normal path of a payment authorization. The 2-second limit\\n  implies a real-time or near-real-time user experience goal for payment\\n  confirmation.\\n\\n- **Implementability contract**\\n  - System boundary: The payment service inbound submission interface and\\n    outbound response interface.\\n  - Responsible system: The payment service.\\n  - Actor: Customer (or a calling system acting on behalf of a customer).\\n  - Affected interfaces: Payment submission interface (inbound); authorization\\n    result interface (outbound).\\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \\\"Valid payment\\\" — **unresolved** (see Q1).\\n    \\\"Authorization result\\\" — **unresolved** (see Q2).\\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the discrete\\n    event of a customer submitting a payment that satisfies the validity\\n    criteria. No precondition beyond validity is stated.\\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal response is an\\n    authorization result returned within 2 seconds. Behavior when the deadline\\n    is exceeded is **unresolved** (see Q4). Behavior on invalid submission is\\n    **unresolved** (see Q5).\\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 2 seconds.\\n    Measurement point is **unresolved** (see Q3). Applicable workload scope is\\n    **unresolved** (see Q6).\\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \\\"Valid payment,\\\"\\n    \\\"authorization result,\\\" measurement point, timeout behavior, and invalid\\n    submission behavior are all unresolved.\\n\\n- **Verification contract**\\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A well-formed, valid payment\\n    request is submitted to the payment service at its inbound interface.\\n    Definition of \\\"valid\\\" must be resolved before the test can be specified.\\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The payment service emits an authorization\\n    result at its outbound response interface. The elapsed time between\\n    receiving the request and emitting the result is recorded.\\n  - Expected result: An authorization result is returned; the elapsed time does\\n    not exceed 2 seconds.\\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if\\n    an authorization result is observable at the service boundary within 2\\n    seconds of receiving a valid payment submission. The exact measurement\\n    points are **unresolved** (see Q3).\\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 2 seconds;\\n    behavior when the deadline is exceeded; behavior on invalid input — all\\n    **unresolved** (see Q4, Q5).\\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence:\\n    Timestamps at the inbound and outbound service boundary; definition of\\n    authorization result structure; agreed workload parameters.\\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes (timing and response content\\n    are both observable at the payment service interface, once terms are\\n    defined).\\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\\n\\n**SR-1 — Failure-path companion (timeout behavior):**\\nRelated to Candidate 1 (timeout on valid payment submission).\\nMissing behavior: What the payment service shall return if the 2-second\\ndeadline is exceeded before an authorization result is available.\\nSuggested draft (provisional, not normative until accepted): \\\"If the payment\\nservice cannot return an authorization result within 2 seconds of receiving a\\nvalid payment submission, then the payment service shall return a timeout\\nerror response to the caller.\\\"\\nReason: Without this, a verifier cannot distinguish a late response from no\\nresponse, and an implementer cannot choose a safe default.\\n\\n**SR-2 — Failure-path companion (invalid payment response):**\\nRelated to Candidate 1 (behavior on invalid payment submission).\\nMissing behavior: What the payment service shall return when the submitted\\npayment is not valid.\\nSuggested draft (provisional): \\\"When a customer submits a payment that does\\nnot satisfy the validity criteria, the payment service shall return a rejection\\nresponse that identifies the reason for rejection.\\\"\\nReason: The source scopes the 2-second obligation to valid payments only;\\nbehavior on invalid submissions is a material gap.\\n\\n**SR-3 — Boundary companion (concurrent workload scope):**\\nRelated to Candidate 1 (workload scope of the timing constraint).\\nMissing behavior: The maximum concurrent load or transaction volume under which\\nthe 2-second guarantee applies.\\nSuggested draft (provisional): \\\"The payment service shall return an\\nauthorization result within 2 seconds for up to [N] concurrent valid payment\\nsubmissions.\\\" ([N] is a placeholder requiring a product or capacity decision.)\\nReason: An unconditional latency guarantee without a workload bound is not\\nmeaningfully verifiable and may not be implementable.\\n\\n**SR-4 — Operational companion (auditability):**\\nRelated to Candidate 1 (observability of authorization results).\\nMissing behavior: Whether authorization results and their associated timing\\nmust be recorded for audit or dispute resolution.\\nSuggested draft (provisional): \\\"The payment service shall record the timestamp\\nof each received valid payment submission and the timestamp of each returned\\nauthorization result.\\\"\\nReason: A 2-second latency requirement implies the ability to measure and\\naudit response time in production; without retained timestamps this may not be\\noperationally verifiable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Consistency Findings\\n\\n**Finding 1 — Gap: No failure path for deadline breach**\\nCandidate 1 defines the normal-path obligation (return within 2 seconds) but\\nis silent on what occurs if the deadline is not met. This leaves both\\nimplementers and verifiers with no normative guidance on timeout behavior.\\nResolution options: (a) add SR-1 as an accepted requirement; (b) confirm that\\nlate results are acceptable and remove the deadline; (c) confirm that the\\ncalling system must handle absence of a response within 2 seconds without a\\nservice-side obligation.\\n\\n**Finding 2 — Gap: Invalid payment behavior undefined**\\nThe trigger condition (\\\"valid payment\\\") implies a complementary case\\n(invalid/unrecognized payment), for which no requirement exists. This is a\\nbehavioral gap, not a conflict.\\nResolution options: (a) accept SR-2; (b) explicitly state that invalid\\nsubmissions are out of scope for this requirement set.\\n\\n**Finding 3 — Potential vacuousness without workload scope**\\nAn unconditional 2-second latency requirement may be infeasible or untestable\\nwithout a defined workload ceiling, making it potentially vacuous in\\nproduction. This is not a conflict among candidates but a concern about the\\nsingle candidate's verifiability.\\nResolution options: (a) accept SR-3 to bound the workload; (b) confirm the\\nrequirement is intended to apply unconditionally and document the implementer's\\nobligation to provision accordingly.\\n\\nNo conflicts, duplicates, or precedence questions exist among the current\\ncandidates because there is only one candidate. The above findings concern\\ncompleteness and verifiability.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n**A1** — \\\"Customer\\\" is interpreted as any principal (human user or calling\\nsystem) that submits a payment to the payment service's submission interface.\\nIf customers and calling systems have distinct behavioral obligations, this\\nassumption must be revisited.\\n\\n**A2** — \\\"Submit\\\" is interpreted as the discrete event of delivering a\\ncomplete payment request to the payment service at its inbound interface. If\\nsubmission is a multi-step process (e.g., initiate, confirm, finalize), the\\ntrigger point must be specified.\\n\\n**A3** — The 2-second limit is interpreted as a maximum elapsed time, not a\\ntarget or a mean. No statistical tolerance (e.g., 99th percentile) is assumed.\\nIf the intent is a percentile-based constraint, the requirement must be\\nrevised.\\n\\n**A4** — \\\"Return\\\" is interpreted as the payment service emitting the\\nauthorization result at its outbound interface to the caller. Network\\ntransmission time beyond the service boundary is not included in the 2-second\\nobligation unless Q3 resolves otherwise.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Rationale and Context\\n\\nThe source sentence establishes a real-time latency guarantee for the\\nauthorization path of a payment service. The 2-second bound is a common\\nindustry target for interactive payment flows, suggesting a user-experience\\nrationale (customers should not wait more than 2 seconds for a payment\\ndecision). However, the source does not state this rationale explicitly.\\n\\nThe requirement is structurally well-formed — it names a system, a trigger,\\nand a response with a timing constraint — but several terms are undefined and\\nfailure paths are absent. These gaps do not change the intent of the source\\nrequest; they are information required to make the requirement implementable\\nand verifiable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Review Status\\n\\n- **Candidate 1:** `needs clarification`\\n  Blocking items: Q1 (definition of \\\"valid payment\\\"), Q2 (definition of\\n  \\\"authorization result\\\"), Q3 (measurement point for 2-second deadline), Q4\\n  (behavior when deadline is exceeded), Q5 (behavior on invalid payment), Q6\\n  (workload scope). The verification contract cannot specify setup, oracle, or\\n  pass/fail criteria until these are resolved.\\n\\n- **SR-1 through SR-4:** Suggested supporting requirements, not yet accepted.\\n  Status will be assigned after the user confirms scope.\\n\\nNo candidates are `ready for review`. All candidates require resolution of the\\nclarifying questions before the readiness gate can be re-evaluated.\\n\"}"},"time":{"start":1791836427812,"end":1791836487418}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1791836487556,"sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","part":{"id":"prt_131c6ff84001D8DHj7j0eTMH54","sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","messageID":"msg_131c610d7001iyvfy6a130dlKs","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"8193e8525c33dbc011e80c8feecfaf47a3ec7325","cost":0.052192800000000004,"tokens":{"input":1,"output":3043,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":18341,"write":278}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1791836488678,"sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","part":{"id":"prt_131c703e6001SjZAjOByNwb5L7","sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","messageID":"msg_131c6ff8a001v53qPA0an7Q7GW","type":"step-start","snapshot":"8193e8525c33dbc011e80c8feecfaf47a3ec7325"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1791836532672,"sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","part":{"id":"prt_131c703ea001Zk7qekBHnlAcB3","sessionID":"ses_ece3a20f8ffeHxmEXDfwVp5Oge","messageID":"msg_131c6ff8a001v53qPA0an7Q7GW","type":"text","text":"---\n\n# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** When a customer submits a payment that the payment service judges to be valid, the payment service must return an authorization result to the caller within 2 seconds.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The payment service — specifically its inbound payment submission interface (the boundary at which a customer or calling system delivers a payment request) and its outbound response (the boundary at which the payment service returns an authorization result).\n\n**Source classification:**\n\n| Source fragment | Kind |\n|---|---|\n| \"When a customer submits a valid payment\" | Trigger / behavioral obligation scope |\n| \"the payment service shall return an authorization result\" | Behavioral obligation |\n| \"within 2 seconds\" | Timing constraint (normative) |\n\nThe source uses `shall`, which is treated as a normative obligation. The phrasing maps directly to the Event-driven EARS pattern. Several terms are undefined or ambiguous and block full readiness.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 — Definition of \"valid payment\":** What conditions must a payment request satisfy to be considered valid? For example: presence of required fields, passing format checks, passing fraud screening, successful authentication, or some combination. This matters because the trigger fires only on a valid payment; the behavior for an invalid submission is currently unspecified.\n\n**Q2 — Definition of \"authorization result\":** What is the observable content or structure of an authorization result? At minimum: does it include an authorization code, an approval/decline indicator, or both? Is a partial result (e.g., a pending status) considered a valid authorization result for this requirement?\n\n**Q3 — Measurement point for the 2-second deadline:** Is the 2 seconds measured from the moment the payment service receives the complete payment request, to the moment the last byte of the authorization result is sent at the service boundary? Or is it measured end-to-end from the customer's perspective? The measurement point is required to make the timing constraint verifiable.\n\n**Q4 — Behavior when the deadline is exceeded:** If the payment service cannot return an authorization result within 2 seconds, what must it do? Options include: return a timeout error response, cancel the authorization attempt, or continue processing and return a late result. The source is silent on this.\n\n**Q5 — Behavior on invalid payment submission:** What shall the payment service return (or do) when a customer submits a payment that is not valid? This is a gap in the source — the 2-second obligation applies only to valid submissions.\n\n**Q6 — Concurrency and workload scope:** Does the 2-second deadline apply at all load levels, or only up to a defined concurrent request rate or transaction volume? An unconditional latency requirement may be infeasible under pathological load; knowing the intended workload scope makes the timing constraint verifiable.\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1\n\n- **Status:** `needs clarification`\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a customer submits a valid payment, the payment service shall return an authorization result within 2 seconds.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source requests a bounded-latency guarantee on the normal path of a payment authorization. The 2-second limit implies a real-time or near-real-time user experience goal for payment confirmation.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The payment service inbound submission interface and outbound response interface.\n  - Responsible system: The payment service.\n  - Actor: Customer (or a calling system acting on behalf of a customer).\n  - Affected interfaces: Payment submission interface (inbound); authorization result interface (outbound).\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \"Valid payment\" — **unresolved** (see Q1). \"Authorization result\" — **unresolved** (see Q2).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the discrete event of a customer submitting a payment that satisfies the validity criteria. No precondition beyond validity is stated.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal response is an authorization result returned within 2 seconds. Behavior when the deadline is exceeded is **unresolved** (see Q4). Behavior on invalid submission is **unresolved** (see Q5).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 2 seconds. Measurement point is **unresolved** (see Q3). Applicable workload scope is **unresolved** (see Q6).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Valid payment,\" \"authorization result,\" measurement point, timeout behavior, and invalid submission behavior are all unresolved.\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: A well-formed, valid payment request is submitted to the payment service at its inbound interface. Definition of \"valid\" must be resolved before the test can be specified.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The payment service emits an authorization result at its outbound response interface. The elapsed time between receiving the request and emitting the result is recorded.\n  - Expected result: An authorization result is returned; the elapsed time does not exceed 2 seconds.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if an authorization result is observable at the service boundary within 2 seconds of receiving a valid payment submission. The exact measurement points are **unresolved** (see Q3).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 2 seconds; behavior when the deadline is exceeded; behavior on invalid input — all **unresolved** (see Q4, Q5).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Timestamps at the inbound and outbound service boundary; definition of authorization result structure; agreed workload parameters.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes (timing and response content are both observable at the payment service interface, once terms are defined).\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**SR-1 — Failure-path companion (timeout behavior):**\nRelated to Candidate 1. Missing behavior: what the payment service shall return if the 2-second deadline is exceeded before an authorization result is available.\nProvisional draft: *\"If the payment service cannot return an authorization result within 2 seconds of receiving a valid payment submission, then the payment service shall return a timeout error response to the caller.\"*\nReason: Without this, a verifier cannot distinguish a late response from no response, and an implementer cannot choose a safe default.\n\n**SR-2 — Failure-path companion (invalid payment response):**\nRelated to Candidate 1. Missing behavior: what the payment service shall return when the submitted payment is not valid.\nProvisional draft: *\"When a customer submits a payment that does not satisfy the validity criteria, the payment service shall return a rejection response that identifies the reason for rejection.\"*\nReason: The source scopes the 2-second obligation to valid payments only; behavior on invalid submissions is a material gap.\n\n**SR-3 — Boundary companion (concurrent workload scope):**\nRelated to Candidate 1. Missing behavior: the maximum concurrent load or transaction volume under which the 2-second guarantee applies.\nProvisional draft: *\"The payment service shall return an authorization result within 2 seconds for up to [N] concurrent valid payment submissions.\"* ([N] is a placeholder requiring a product or capacity decision.)\nReason: An unconditional latency guarantee without a workload bound is not meaningfully verifiable and may not be implementable.\n\n**SR-4 — Operational companion (auditability):**\nRelated to Candidate 1. Missing behavior: whether authorization results and their associated timing must be recorded for audit or dispute resolution.\nProvisional draft: *\"The payment service shall record the timestamp of each received valid payment submission and the timestamp of each returned authorization result.\"*\nReason: A 2-second latency requirement implies the ability to measure and audit response time in production; without retained timestamps this may not be operationally verifiable.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n**Finding 1 — Gap: No failure path for deadline breach**\nCandidate 1 defines the normal-path obligation but is silent on what occurs if the 2-second deadline is not met. This leaves both implementers and verifiers without normative guidance on timeout behavior.\nResolution options: (a) accept SR-1; (b) confirm that late results are acceptable and remove the deadline; (c) confirm the calling system must handle the absence of a response without a service-side timeout obligation.\n\n**Finding 2 — Gap: Invalid payment behavior undefined**\nThe trigger condition (\"valid payment\") implies a complementary case (invalid submission), for which no requirement exists.\nResolution options: (a) accept SR-2; (b) explicitly state that invalid submissions are out of scope for this requirement set.\n\n**Finding 3 — Potential vacuousness without workload scope**\nAn unconditional 2-second latency requirement may be infeasible or untestable without a defined workload ceiling, making it potentially vacuous in production.\nResolution options: (a) accept SR-3 to bound the workload; (b) confirm the requirement is intended to apply unconditionally and document the implementer's obligation to provision accordingly.\n\nNo conflicts, duplicates, or precedence questions exist among candidates — there is only one candidate. The findings above concern completeness and verifiability.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1** — \"Customer\" is interpreted as any principal (human user or calling system) that submits a payment to the payment service's submission interface. If customers and calling systems have distinct obligations, this must be revisited.\n\n**A2** — \"Submit\" is interpreted as the discrete event of delivering a complete payment request to the payment service. If submission is multi-step (initiate, confirm, finalize), the trigger point must be specified.\n\n**A3** — The 2-second limit is interpreted as a maximum elapsed time, not a target or mean. No statistical tolerance (e.g., 99th percentile) is assumed. If a percentile-based constraint is intended, the requirement must be revised.\n\n**A4** — \"Return\" is interpreted as the payment service emitting the authorization result at its outbound interface to the caller. Network transmission time beyond the service boundary is not included in the 2-second obligation unless Q3 resolves otherwise.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe source sentence establishes a real-time latency guarantee for the authorization path of a payment service. The 2-second bound is a common industry target for interactive payment flows, suggesting a user-experience rationale. However, the source does not state this rationale explicitly.\n\nThe requirement is structurally well-formed — it names a system, a trigger, and a timed response — but several terms are undefined and failure paths are absent. These gaps do not change the intent of the source request; they are information required to make the requirement implementable and verifiable.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n- **Candidate 1:** `needs clarification`\n  Blocking items: Q1 (definition of \"valid payment\"), Q2 (definition of \"authorization result\"), Q3 (measurement point for the 2-second deadline), Q4 (behavior when the deadline is exceeded), Q5 (behavior on invalid payment submission), Q6 (applicable workload scope). The verification contract cannot specify setup, oracle, or pass/fail criteria until these are resolved.\n\n- **SR-1 through SR-4:** Suggested supporting requirements, not yet accepted. Status will be assigned after scope is confirmed.\n\nNo candidates are `ready for review`. All require resolution of the clarifying questions before the readiness gate can be re-evaluated.\n\n---\n\nThe package has also been written to `artifacts/result.md`.","time":{"start":1791836488682,"end":1791836532672}}}
